📘 20 Senior‑Level Linux Internals Interview Questions (Concise Answers)
Processes & Syscalls
Explain fork vs exec.

fork() → creates child process (copy).

exec() → replaces process image with new program.

What is a zombie process?

Process finished but parent hasn’t read exit status. Entry remains in process table.

Difference between SIGTERM and SIGKILL.

SIGTERM → graceful termination (catchable).

SIGKILL → force kill (uncatchable).

How does strace help debugging?

Traces syscalls, shows file/network access, signals.

Explain context switch.

Kernel saves CPU state of one process, loads another. Costly if frequent.

Memory Management
What is virtual memory?

Abstraction: maps process memory to physical RAM + swap.

Explain page cache.

Kernel caches disk pages in RAM for faster I/O.

What is swappiness?

Kernel parameter controlling tendency to swap (0 = avoid, 100 = aggressive).

Difference between hugepages and THP.

Hugepages → manually allocated large pages.

Transparent Hugepages → kernel auto‑manages.

How to debug memory leaks?

Tools: valgrind, pmap, /proc/<pid>/smaps.

Kernel & Scheduling
Explain CFS scheduler.

Fair scheduling based on virtual runtime. Balances CPU across tasks.

What is nice value?

User‑set priority hint (−20 = highest, +19 = lowest).

Difference between real‑time and normal scheduling.

Real‑time (FIFO, RR) → strict priority.

Normal → fair share via CFS.

How to debug slow boot times?

systemd-analyze, journalctl -b.

Explain kernel panic.

Fatal error in kernel, system halts. Debug via logs, crash dumps.

Filesystems & I/O
What is an inode?

Metadata structure storing file attributes, not name.

Difference between ext4 and XFS.

ext4 → general purpose, journaling.

XFS → high‑performance, large files.

How does journaling work?

Logs metadata changes before commit → crash recovery.

What is lsof used for?

Lists open files, sockets, pipes per process.

How to debug I/O wait?

Tools: iotop, pidstat -d, blktrace.


Day 3 Troubleshooting Lab Script (WSL/Ubuntu)
#!/bin/bash
# Day 3 - Linux Internals Troubleshooting Lab

echo "=== CPU Spike Simulation ==="
stress --cpu 4 --timeout 30 &   # simulate load
CPU_PID=$!
echo "Stress PID: $CPU_PID"

sleep 5
echo "=== Monitor with top/htop ==="
top -b -n 1 | head -20

echo "=== PID CPU usage ==="
pidstat -p $CPU_PID 1 5

echo "=== Syscall tracing ==="
strace -p $CPU_PID -c -q -w -o strace_output.txt &
sleep 5
kill -9 $CPU_PID
echo "Stress process killed."

echo "=== Memory Debugging ==="
echo "Free memory snapshot:"
free -h
echo "VMStat snapshot:"
vmstat 1 5

echo "=== Open Files ==="
lsof -p $$ | head -10

echo "=== Network Sockets ==="
ss -tulpn | head -10

echo "=== Perf Profiling (short run) ==="
perf stat sleep 2

echo "=== Sysdig Capture (requires sudo) ==="
sudo sysdig -n 10 evt.type=open

echo "Lab complete. Check strace_output.txt for syscall summary."

